@brief
    PAY-455  Support want to refund part of an order instead of all of
             it. The column landed last sprint and the test factory was
             updated with it, which is why the build is currently red:
             `refundedTotal` exists in the database and in the factory
             and nowhere in the TypeScript.

             Take it the rest of the way through the stack.

Six files, in the order they are open.

  1. order.entity.ts — `refundedTotal: number;` directly under `total`.

  2. order.repository.ts — map it in `toEntity`, directly under `total`.

  3. errors/default.json — `REFUND_EXCEEDS_TOTAL`, a 409, not sent to
     Sentry, directly under `ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED`.

  4. order.provider.ts — a `refundPartial(id: string, amount: number)`
     below `cancel`, one blank line between them:

         const order = await this.findOne(id);
         refuse with REFUND_EXCEEDS_TOTAL when
           order.refundedTotal + amount > order.total
         await this.paymentService.refund(order.id, amount);
         return the repository update with
           values: { refundedTotal: order.refundedTotal + amount }

  5. order.resolver.ts — `refundOrder` below `cancelOrder`, taking
     `args: { id: string; amount: number }` and calling
     `this.orderProvider.refundPartial(args.id, args.amount)`.

  6. order.provider.test.ts — one more `it`, below the existing one,
     named `refuses to refund more than the order total`. Build the
     order with `orderFactory({ total: 5000, refundedTotal: 4000 })`,
     stub `findOne` the same way, and expect
     `provider.refundPartial(order.id, 2000)` to reject with
     `REFUND_EXCEEDS_TOTAL`.

The factory is open for reference and does not change. Neither does the
migration under prisma/.

Par is 830 keystrokes. Typing the four new blocks out by hand runs well
over a thousand. Every one of the new blocks is a near copy of something
already in the file it belongs to.

@keys
| `15Y` then `p` | yank a method with its blank line, put it back below |
| `ciw` | change the word under the cursor |
| `ci(` `ci{` `ci'` `ci"` | change inside the brackets, braces, quotes |
| `f)` `i` | append just before the closing bracket |
| `:s/old/new/g` | this line only |
| `:bn` `:wqa` | next buffer, write everything |

@hints
Work in the order the buffers are open. That order is not an accident:
every file tells you the name you need in the next one, and the test is
last because it exercises everything above it.
--
Four of the six edits are duplicate-and-edit. The entity's new field is
the `total` line copied and one word changed. The JSON is the
ALREADY_SHIPPED line copied and one string changed. The repository line
is a copy with two words changed, which is `:s/total/refundedTotal/g`
over that one line. The resolver method is `cancelOrder` copied with
three edits.
--
For the provider: put the cursor on the blank line above `cancel` and
yank fifteen lines — the gap and the whole method. Move to the closing
brace of `cancel` and put. You now have a correctly spaced second method
whose body is already the right shape, and five small edits to make on
it.
--
Edit the copy by searching forwards. Every string you need to change
occurs first in the original and second in the copy, and you are sitting
below the original, so `/SHIPPED` `Enter` goes straight to the copy's
copy of it. This is why editing the lower duplicate is easier than
editing the upper one.

@solution
A route that measures 723 keystrokes. It is long, but there are only two
ideas in it.

**entity** `/total: number` `Enter` `yyp` `ciwrefundedTotal` `Esc`

**repository** `:bn` `/total: record` `Enter` `yyp` `:s/total/refundedTotal/g`
— two words on one line, and a line-scoped substitution is shorter than
two `ciw`s and cannot land on the wrong one.

**errors** `:bn` `/ALREADY_SHIPPED` `Enter` `yyp` `ci"REFUND_EXCEEDS_TOTAL`
`Esc` — the status code and the Sentry flag were already right, which is
the whole argument for copying the neighbouring line instead of typing a
new one.

**provider** `:bn` `/public async cancel` `Enter` `k` `15Y` `14j` `p`.
The `k` is the important keystroke: it puts the blank line into the yank,
so the copy arrives correctly spaced and you never type or count a blank
line. Then, on the copy:

    www ciwrefundPartial Esc         the method name
    f) i, amount: number Esc         the second parameter
    /=== 'SHIPPED' Enter             jumps to the copy, not the original
    ci(order.refundedTotal + amount > order.total Esc
    /ALREADY_SHIPPED Enter  ci'REFUND_EXCEEDS_TOTAL Esc
    /order.id) Enter  ci(order.id, amount Esc
    /status: 'CANCELLED' Enter
    ci{ refundedTotal: order.refundedTotal + amount  Esc

**resolver** `:bn` `/cancelOrder` `Enter` `k` `4Y` `3j` `p`, then
`www` `ciwrefundOrder` `Esc`, `f{` `ci{ id: string; amount: number ` `Esc`,
`/cancel(args` `Enter` `ciwrefundPartial` `Esc` `f)` `i, args.amount` `Esc`.

**test** `:bn` `/refuses to cancel` `Enter` `k` `7Y` `6j` `p`, then on the
copy: `f'` `ci'refuses to refund more than the order total` `Esc`,
`j0f{` `ci{ total: 5000, refundedTotal: 4000 ` `Esc`,
`/cancel(order` `Enter` `ciwrefundPartial` `Esc` `f)` `i, 2000` `Esc`
`f'` `ci'REFUND_EXCEEDS_TOTAL` `Esc`.

Then `:wqa`.

Two ideas, then.

**Copy the neighbour.** Every new block in this ticket already exists
four lines away with two or three words different. Copying it brings the
indentation, the spacing, the trailing commas and the house style with
it, free and correct. Typing it brings your best recollection of the
house style. On a six-file ticket the difference is not the keystrokes,
it is the three review comments you do not get.

**Search forwards into the copy.** Once the copy is below the original,
every distinctive string in it has exactly one occurrence between you and
it. So `/SHIPPED` `Enter` is a reliable "go to the next thing I have to
change" and you never look at a line number. That is what makes the edit
sequence above readable as a list of decisions rather than as navigation.

The thing that separates this from the same work in an editor with a
mouse is not speed. It is that at no point did you scroll, and at no
point did you retype something that already existed. Those two habits are
what the last thirty-one chapters were for.

